WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.016
Negating a quantity statement means finding exactly what makes it false.

2
00:00:05.316 --> 00:00:09.156
The negation need not make the strongest possible opposing claim.

3
00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:11.568
Most of these four files are indexed.

4
00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:15.068
Three indexed files would be a majority; two would not.

5
00:00:15.368 --> 00:00:19.571
So exactly two indexed and two unindexed already makes most false.

6
00:00:19.871 --> 00:00:22.196
It does not make most unindexed true either.

7
00:00:22.496 --> 00:00:26.123
The two majority claims can both be false at the halfway point.

8
00:00:26.423 --> 00:00:31.735
A negation test fails if you make the candidate much stronger than its actual denial.

9
00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:36.685
Count boundaries precisely before using the result to evaluate an argument.

10
00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:38.884
For all, every member must fit.

11
00:00:39.184 --> 00:00:40.699
For some, one is enough.

12
00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:45.095
For most, the number must be strictly above half of the same reference group.

13
00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:48.019
Try the smallest change that defeats the claim.

14
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:55.657
If one exception or an exact tie is enough, keep that case available instead of strengthening the negation for convenience.

15
00:00:55.957 --> 00:01:00.267
With five files, most needs three; denying most permits two, one or none.
